From b1df3131d177e960d9aa860fbb22c7f7c70bea56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 21:06:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Read a table key name as ABAP does: primary_key in any case,
 no padding

The runtime compared key names verbatim, so every name ABAP accepts for
the primary key other than lower-case "primary_key" was taken for a
secondary key and threw "Table, secondary key ... not found":

- LOOP AT t USING KEY PRIMARY_KEY - the name as written in upper case,
  and a dynamic USING KEY ( name ) holding PRIMARY_KEY
- READ TABLE t WITH [TABLE] KEY primary_key COMPONENTS ..., which the
  transpiler hands over as keyName: "primary_key" and readTable looked up
  among the secondary keys
- a dynamic name in a c field, which comes with the field's padding
  ("K                   "), for the primary and the secondary keys alike

secondaryKeyName( ) now reads a name the way ABAP does - trailing blanks
dropped, primary_key in any case (or an initial name) meaning the primary
key - and LOOP, READ TABLE and the secondary-key lookups use it. A LOOP
USING KEY primary_key is then the same loop as one without USING KEY,
also in visiting the rows its body appends, which it did not before.
